---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import NavBar from '../components/NavBar.astro';
---

<BaseLayout title="Mission" description="amber mission and vision — why this exists">
  <NavBar />
  <main>
    <h1>Mission & Vision</h1>

    <section class="section">
      <p>
        amber exists because the AI agent space is full of Python wrappers around OpenAI's API,
        Node.js scripts, and React frontends with Flask backends. None of them are built for
        the reality of Linux servers with real work to do.
      </p>
    </section>

    <hr class="divider-thick" />

    <section class="section">
      <h2>Mission</h2>
      <p>
        Build a bare-metal C++ agent harness that runs on Linux servers, connects to LLMs,
        executes shell commands, reads files, and compresses context — all in one process,
        zero Python dependency.
      </p>
    </section>

    <hr class="divider" />

    <section class="section">
      <h2>Vision</h2>
      <p>
        A terminal-native agent runtime that feels like it was built by someone who has spent
        28 years in tech. Not a SaaS product. Not a chatbot. A tool for people who understand
        what happens when you run <code>rm -rf</code> at 3 AM.
      </p>
    </section>

    <hr class="divider" />

    <section class="section">
      <h2>What We Believe</h2>
      <ul class="feature-list">
        <li><strong>Compiled code matters.</strong> C++17 with libcurl, ncurses, zero Python dependency.</li>
        <li><strong>Terminal is a feature.</strong> BitchX-inspired IRC-style UI, not a web frontend.</li>
        <li><strong>Real benchmarking.</strong> amber-bench with KPI aggregation, not marketing numbers.</li>
        <li><strong>Compression that works.</strong> Classify-then-extract with live UI progress.</li>
        <li><strong>Open source.</strong> Apache 2.0. Fork it, modify it, make it yours.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
